[27.218429, "o", "bash /opt/gnomon-run.sh 'There'\"'\"'s something wrong with my system-wide python installation - I can'\"'\"'t seem to install packages with pip.'; tmux wait -S done\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[89.361944, "o", "On PATH the default interpreter is `/usr/local/bin/python3` → **Python 3.13.1**. Its `site-packages` contained the pip *metadata* directory `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` (and the `pip`/`pip3`/`pip3.13` entry-point scripts in `/usr/local/bin`), but the actual `pip/` package directory was **gone**:\r\n\r\n- `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/RECORD` lists **841** files under `pip/`\r\n- files actually present under `site-packages`: **10** (just `README.txt` + the dist-info files)\r\n\r\nSo the launcher scripts still existed and still pointed at a real interpreter, which is exactly why the failure looked like \"pip is broken\" rather than \"pip is missing\":\r\n\r\n```\r\nFile \"/usr/local/bin/pip\", line 5, in <module>\r\n    from pip._internal.cli.main import main\r\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\r\n```\r\n\r\n`python3 -m pip` failed identically. This is the classic fingerprint of a `pip uninstall pip` or a botched cleanup — the dist-info survived, the code did not. Note also that `/usr/bin/python3` (Debian's 3.11.2) had no pip either, so there"]
[89.362156, "o", " was no working fallback.\r\n\r\n## The fix\r\n\r\nNetwork is disabled in this sandbox, so I used the offline path. Python 3.13 ships its own pip wheel at `ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`.\r\n\r\n1. Moved the orphaned `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` to `/tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.orphaned.bak` — otherwise pip's installer can see the stale metadata and consider the requirement already satisfied, reinstalling nothing.\r\n2. Ran `python3 -m ensurepip --default-pip`, which installed pip 24.3.1 from the bundled wheel.\r\n\r\n## Verification\r\n\r\n- `pip --version`, `python3 -m pip --version`, `pip3.13 --version` → all report `pip 24.3.1 ... (python 3.13)`\r\n- `import pip, pip._internal.cli.main` → ok\r\n- **RECORD audit: 853 entries present, 0 missing** (was 841 missing)\r\n- Real install test: built a local wheel, `pip install --no-index` → succeeded, `import demo_pkg` → `42`, `pip show` → correct metadata\r\n- Uninstall test: `pip uninstall -y` → succeeded, module no longer importable\r\n\r\nTest artifacts cleaned up. The b"]
[89.362214, "o", "ackup of the orphaned dist-info is still at `/tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.orphaned.bak` if you want to diff it; it's safe to delete.\r\n\r\nTwo things worth knowing:\r\n\r\n- **Network is off in this session**, so I could not verify against PyPI. Real package installs will need `network = true` in `.gnomon/policy.toml` (or `/network on`). The installer itself is proven working — that's a separate switch.\r\n- **You're running as root.** pip installed into system `site-packages` and warns about it. For actual work, `python3 -m venv .venv` is the safer habit; `venv` is confirmed importable. Also, if a distro-packaged pip gets reinstalled later over `/usr/local`, that's a separate tree — `/usr/bin/python3` (3.11) still has no pip at all, and fixing it on Debian would be `apt install python3-pip` (needs network).\r\n\r\nOne loose end I did not chase: whatever deleted that directory. If something has a habit of clearing `site-packages`, this will recur.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=17 surface=8"]
